          <p>
            Oil is the stain people most often underestimate. A degreaser removes fresh drips, but a stain that has been there for months has moved into the concrete, and a cleaner only reaches the top. For those, a poultice or enzyme treatment is the right tool, and our ranking of the <a href="/best-oil-stain-remover-for-concrete/">best oil stain removers for concrete</a> covers them in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Take a few phone photos before you start, in the same light and from the same spots. Concrete changes color dramatically between wet and dry, and memory is unreliable after an afternoon of scrubbing. Comparing dry photos a day later tells you honestly which stains are gone, which faded, and which need a different product rather than another round of the same one.
          </p>
