        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Driveway Cleaner Chemistry Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Alkaline degreasers</strong> use high pH and surfactants to emulsify oil so it can be rinsed away. They are the strongest option for petroleum stains and tire marks. Strong versions can dull aluminum, irritate skin and haze some acrylic sealers, so handle them carefully and rinse thoroughly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Oxygenated cleaners</strong> release active oxygen, typically from sodium percarbonate or similar compounds, which breaks down organic stains such as leaf tannins, algae and mildew. They are the reason a shaded driveway can go from gray-green to pale in one wash. They are gentler on plants than chlorine bleach and leave no chlorine smell, though they still need rinsing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Acid cleaners</strong> dissolve minerals: rust, efflorescence and hard-water deposits. Dedicated rust removers are formulated to dissolve iron oxide with less risk to the concrete than straight muriatic acid, which etches cement paste aggressively. Acids should never be mixed with bleach or other cleaners, and they need careful rinsing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Biocide treatments</strong> such as Wet and Forget kill organic growth and let weather wash it away over time. They do not clean in the conventional sense, which is why results are slow, but they keep growth from returning as quickly. Chlorine bleach mixtures also kill growth quickly but are harsher on plants, metal and some surfaces, and must never be combined with ammonia or acids.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NEVER MIX</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Bleach plus acid or ammonia releases toxic gas.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Use one chemical type at a time, rinse thoroughly between them, and store products separately. If you plan to follow a bleach or oxygen treatment with a rust remover, rinse the surface completely and let it dry first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Concrete, Asphalt and Pavers Need Different Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most driveway cleaners are formulated for concrete, which tolerates high pH, oxidizers and diluted acids well. The main risks on concrete are etching from strong acids, which roughens the surface and exposes sand, and uneven lightening when you clean only some areas. Colored, stamped and sealed concrete are more sensitive, because some cleaners haze or lift acrylic sealers and acids can shift color. Always test on a hidden corner first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Asphalt is a petroleum-bound surface, and it reacts badly to solvents and very strong degreasers, which soften the binder. Water-based cleaners at moderate dilution, oxygen cleaners and biocide treatments are generally fine, but read each label for asphalt guidance. Acids are usually unnecessary on blacktop because rust shows less on a dark surface. The biggest asphalt risk is pressure: aging blacktop loses fine aggregate under a narrow tip. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing driveway oil stains from asphalt and concrete</a> covers blacktop in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers combine concrete or clay units with sand joints. The units take concrete cleaners, but the joints are the weak point: high pressure blows out joint sand, and organic growth often roots in the joints. Use moderate pressure, a surface cleaner rather than a narrow wand tip, and plan to refill joints with sand after a deep clean. If your pavers are sealed, check the sealer's compatibility with the cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brick driveways and aggregate finishes deserve extra caution with acids, which can dissolve mortar and loosen exposed stone. Stick to oxygen cleaners and degreasers on those, and use rust removers only as spot treatments with careful rinsing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Pump Sprayer, Brush or Pressure Washer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pump sprayer is the most versatile applicator. It lays down cleaner at the strength you mixed, which matters because pressure washer detergent systems dilute heavily. A two-gallon garden sprayer with a fan nozzle covers a driveway quickly. Use a dedicated sprayer for cleaners and do not reuse one that held herbicide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure washers apply detergent in two ways. Most consumer units have an onboard tank or a downstream siphon that only draws soap at low pressure, through a black soap nozzle. That dilutes the cleaner substantially, often to a small fraction of its bottle strength, so choose products labeled for pressure washer use and expect less punch than a sprayed application. Some products are sold pre-diluted for this purpose, and using a heavy concentrate in a soap tank can be wasteful.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brushes still matter. A stiff nylon push broom or deck brush works cleaner into texture and breaks up oil film far better than spraying alone. For spot stains, brush; for whole-surface washing, spray and let dwell. The rinse is where pressure earns its keep, and a rotating attachment gives far more even results than a wand. Our guide to the <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">best surface cleaners for pressure washers</a> explains how to match one to your machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not own a pressure washer, a garden hose with a high-flow nozzle and a stiff broom can still produce good results with oxygen cleaners and degreasers; it just takes longer and leaves a slightly less even finish. If you are shopping for a machine, our <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">pressure washer guide</a> covers what PSI and GPM a driveway job needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the applicator, test your dilution on a small, out-of-the-way section first. Spray, let it dwell for the label time, rinse, and let it dry. If the result is good, keep that mix for the whole job. If it is weak, step up the concentration within the label range before buying a different product; many disappointing results come from cleaner diluted far below what the job needed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whole-wash" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Wash a Whole Driveway, Start to Finish
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pick a cool, overcast day or work early in the morning. Cleaners that dry on hot concrete stop working and can leave residue or streaks. Clear the driveway, sweep loose debris, and cover or wet down nearby plants. Block the storm drain or plan to direct rinse water onto lawn or gravel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pre-treat specialty stains first: degreaser and a brush on oil spots, rust remover on orange stains, both rinsed thoroughly before the general wash. Then apply the general cleaner over the whole slab, working from the bottom of the slope up so cleaner running downhill does not create clean streaks on dry concrete. Keep it wet for the dwell time on the label, misting as needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse from the top down with a surface cleaner, overlapping passes by about a third of the deck width, then finish edges and corners with a wand at a wide angle. Rinse the whole driveway again with a wide fan to flush residue. Let it dry fully, which takes most of a day, then look for spots that need a second round. Stains always look better wet.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Whole-Driveway Wash at a Glance</p>
