        <div id="specialty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Rust, Fertilizer and Mildew: The Stubborn Three
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rust is the stain that most often makes people think their cleaner failed. It is a mineral, so degreasers and oxygen cleaners slide right over it. Use a dedicated rust remover such as F9 BARC as a spot treatment: wet the area, apply, brush, rinse thoroughly. Sprinkler rust will return unless you fix the source, usually iron-rich well water. Adjusting sprinkler heads so they do not spray the driveway is the permanent fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fertilizer stains look like small orange or brown dots scattered near lawn edges. Many lawn fertilizers contain iron, which oxidizes on wet concrete. Blow or sweep stray granules off hard surfaces right after spreading, before irrigation or rain. Treat existing spots with the same acid-based rust remover.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mildew and algae thrive where concrete stays damp and shaded. An oxygen cleaner washes off current growth quickly, while a biocide such as Wet and Forget kills what remains and slows regrowth. Trimming back branches and improving drainage helps more than any chemical. The same growth on asphalt is handled similarly, and our guide on <a href="/how-to-remove-moss-from-asphalt-driveway/">removing moss from an asphalt driveway</a> covers blacktop-specific steps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tire marks, especially from hot tires turning into the garage, are a mix of rubber and plasticizers that can migrate into sealers. A degreaser and stiff brush usually lifts them from bare concrete. On sealed concrete, some tire marks are actually in the sealer and will not come out without stripping that area, which is a sealer selection issue rather than a cleaning one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cleaning Before You Seal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every sealer manufacturer requires a clean, dry surface, and cleaning is the single biggest factor in whether a sealer bonds and lasts. Oil, mildew and dirt film all interfere with adhesion. On concrete, residual oil causes sealers to peel or spot; on asphalt, oil spots need an oil spot primer before sealer goes on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan the timeline backward from your sealing date. Clean at least one or two days ahead so the slab can dry completely, longer for penetrating sealers that need deep pores to be dry. Many labels specify a minimum dry time after washing and a rain-free window after application. Rinse especially thoroughly, because cleaner residue can interfere with sealer bonding as much as dirt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test readiness with a water sprinkle: clean, dry concrete absorbs water evenly and darkens. Beading means a sealer, oil or residue is still there. For guidance on the sealing step itself, see <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/">how to seal a concrete driveway</a>, and for blacktop, our guide to the <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best asphalt driveway sealers</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are cleaning only for appearance, not sealing, you can skip the extended dry time, but the rest of the process is the same. A thorough wash every year or two keeps grime from building to the point where it needs aggressive treatment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Coverage, Dilution and Real Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Comparing driveway cleaners by bottle price is misleading. A concentrate that dilutes 1 to 10 makes ten times the working solution of a ready-to-use spray at the same price. Read the label for dilution and coverage. Pressure washer concentrates are typically formulated so one gallon handles a standard driveway; spot products like rust removers are measured in stains, not square feet.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical kit for a typical concrete driveway is a gallon of general wash at roughly $10 to $25, a degreaser concentrate at roughly $15 to $30 if you have oil, and a rust remover only if you see orange. That is roughly $25 to $90 at the time of writing, and most of it lasts several cleanings. A biocide for shaded areas adds roughly $25 to $45 but needs reapplying only occasionally.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional driveway cleaning is priced by square footage and region, and many companies bundle oil spot treatment. It makes sense for very large driveways, heavy staining, or if you do not want to buy or rent a pressure washer. For an average driveway that just needs a wash, DIY costs a fraction of a professional visit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not overspend on products for stains you do not have. If your driveway is simply dull, a general wash and a surface cleaner rinse will deliver most of the visible improvement. Specialty products are worth buying only when you can see the stain they target.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Plants, Pets and Runoff
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wet down lawn edges and plant beds before you start and rinse them again afterward. Water-saturated foliage absorbs less cleaner. Cover delicate plants with plastic during application, removing it promptly so plants do not overheat in sun. Oxygen cleaners and biodegradable degreasers are gentler than chlorine and high-pH products, but none are meant to soak flower beds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep pets and children off the driveway until it is rinsed and dry. Alkaline and acid cleaners irritate paws and skin, and some biocides need time to dry before contact. Store concentrates out of reach in their original containers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storm drains in most communities send water directly to creeks and lakes, so wash water with oil and cleaner residue should not go into them. Many towns prohibit it. Direct runoff to gravel or lawn where allowed, berm the drain with a towel or sandbag, or collect water with a wet vacuum for disposal. Collected oily water belongs at a household hazardous waste site.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, protect yourself. Wear chemical-resistant gloves and safety glasses when mixing and spraying, closed shoes when pressure washing, and hearing protection with gas machines. Pressure washer streams can cut skin; never point the wand at people or pets.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying one cleaner for every stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Degreasers do not dissolve rust, oxygen cleaners barely touch old oil, and rust removers do nothing for mildew. A driveway with several stain types needs a short kit. Identify what you see, then buy one product per stain family instead of hoping a single bottle covers everything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting cleaner dry on hot concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most cleaners stop working once they dry, and dried residue can leave streaks and blotches that are harder to remove than the original grime. Work on a cool or overcast day, apply in sections, and mist the surface to keep it wet through the full dwell time on the label.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spot cleaning without a whole wash</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cleaning only the oil spots leaves bright patches surrounded by dull, dirty concrete, which often looks worse than before. Treat spots first, then run a general wash over the entire driveway so the color evens out. On asphalt, sealing after spot cleaning achieves the same result.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using muriatic acid as an all-purpose cleaner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Straight muriatic acid etches concrete, exposes sand, can discolor stamped finishes and releases harsh fumes. It also does nothing for oil. Use a formulated rust remover only on rust and mineral stains, and use degreasers and oxygen cleaners for everything else.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Washing residue into the storm drain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Runoff from driveway cleaning carries oil, cleaner and debris straight to local waterways in most areas, and many towns restrict it. Block or berm the drain, direct water onto lawn or gravel where permitted, or collect it with a wet vacuum for proper disposal.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

