        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which driveway cleaner should I buy first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with a general pressure washer concentrate, such as Zep's, if the driveway is mostly dull and dirty. Add a degreaser like Oil Eater if you have oil spots, a rust remover if you see orange stains, and a biocide like Wet and Forget for shaded mildew. Buying by stain type gets better results than one all-purpose bottle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will dish soap or household detergent clean a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dish soap is a mild degreaser and works on fresh oil spots, but it is weak for a whole driveway and can leave residue if not rinsed well. Laundry detergents containing oxygen bleach can brighten organic stains. Purpose-made driveway cleaners are designed for concrete, pressure washer dilution and outdoor rinsing, so they are usually more efficient.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is oxygen bleach better than chlorine bleach for driveways?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, yes. Oxygen cleaners break down organic stains like tannins and mildew, are gentler on plants and metal, and do not create chlorine fumes. Chlorine bleach kills growth faster but is harsher on landscaping and can never be mixed with ammonia or acids. Neither removes oil or rust.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I get rust stains off a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use an acid-based rust remover formulated for concrete, such as F9 BARC. Wet the area, apply the product, brush it in, and rinse thoroughly. Test on colored or sealed concrete first. Then fix the source, usually sprinkler water with high iron or fertilizer granules left on the slab, or the stains will return.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are driveway cleaners safe on asphalt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many water-based cleaners are, but not all. Solvent-based degreasers and very strong alkaline products can soften asphalt binder. Look for asphalt on the label, test a small corner, and keep pressure moderate, because a narrow tip can strip aggregate from older blacktop. Biocides and oxygen cleaners are generally asphalt-friendly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I clean my driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A general wash once a year keeps most driveways looking good and prevents buildup that needs aggressive treatment. Treat oil spots as they happen, ideally within an hour. Shaded driveways prone to mildew may benefit from a biocide treatment once or twice a year. Clean thoroughly before every sealing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a pressure washer to clean a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, but it helps. A pump sprayer, stiff push broom and garden hose with a high-flow nozzle can clean a driveway well, especially with oxygen cleaners. A pressure washer with a surface cleaner attachment rinses faster and leaves a more uniform finish, which matters most before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

