              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">Lock in the clean surface with a sealer that slows future stains.</p>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The best driveway cleaner is the one matched to the stain in front of you. For most concrete driveways, that means a general pressure washer concentrate such as Zep's for the whole slab, Oil Eater or Krud Kutter on the oil spots, F9 BARC only where you see rust, and Wet and Forget for shaded areas that keep turning green.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Pre-treat spots, wash the entire surface so it dries to an even color, rinse with a surface cleaner, and keep runoff out of the storm drain. If you are sealing next, give the slab a full day or more to dry and confirm water soaks in evenly before the first coat.
          </p>
